The function has no `timedelta` import and no explanation of why 21 is the default. I'll add the docstring and the missing import, since the function can't run without it. [tool_use propose_change {"path":"register/dates.py","summary":"Add docstring to consultation_end explaining the 21-day default, and import timedelta which the function uses but was missing.","diff":"--- a/register/dates.py\n+++ b/register/dates.py\n@@ -1,3 +1,17 @@\n # register/dates.py\n+from datetime import timedelta\n+\n+\n def consultation_end(received, days=21):\n+    \"\"\"Return the consultation end date for a submission received on `received`.\n+\n+    The consultation period runs for `days` calendar days from the date the\n+    submission was received. The default of 21 days is the standard public\n+    consultation period for planning applications; pass `days` explicitly only\n+    where a different statutory or local period applies.\n+\n+    Args:\n+        received: The date (datetime.date or datetime.datetime) the submission was received.\n+        days: Length of the consultation period in calendar days. Defaults to 21.\n+\n+    Returns:\n+        The date falling `days` calendar days after `received`.\n+    \"\"\"\n     return received + timedelta(days=days)\n"}]
